that he wanted to get done but didn't  lyndon johnson undressed in front of you  one night  well that was another  quite funny story because i was at his  34th wedding anniversary  and  just as we were he was in a pretty bad  mood anyway  it was during the vietnam war  and  afterwards we went upstairs and he just  went to bed and left us and  his bedroom oddly was right next door to  the upstairs living room the yellow room  and there were some double doors between  and suddenly he came out we were saying  good night to mrs johnson and the doors  were flung open and he came out and said  come here and i looked  over my shoulder hoping somebody was  there but he was saying it to me  and then he said come here to abe fortis  who was then i believe on the supreme  court  and um  we went into his bedroom  and the post was lying on the bed  and  to  a man called to briner was the chief  commissioner in the district with still  three  but he was kind of like the appointed  mayor  and he we had been forming it back to  him so he was our from the johnson point  of view it was our creation  and  he had appointed a police commissioner  without consulting the president which  he had told him to do  and he was very displeased because he  wanted to prove something about being  anti-crime by appointing a super police  commissioner so he was really mad that  briner had gone ahead and done this and  he started yelling at me about our  goddamn mayor and he was this idea and  it was our fault he'd appointed this man  hadn't asked him when he you kind of  consulted him and he wanted to do it  and he started to undress  and i  i mean i was this is 64 please  and i was new to the job i'd only gone  to work and he's flinging his clothes  off while he's balling me out  and i thought wait a minute what is  going to happen here can this be me am i  standing here in the president's bedroom  and what is going to happen he suddenly  said turn around  and so i turned around and went right on  and finally he said all right good night  and  abe and i both left  and i mean it was quite a beginning  richard nixon  i never knew him really  um personally  uh i  met him and i talked to him and i even  interviewed him on and he came to lunch  at the paper an editorial lunch  so i saw him a little bit  but i didn't really know him personally  and  i think that he had many good traits i  think he was a real jekyll hyde  character because he had all these  things that we see keep seeing coming  out on the tapes and this really low  level side to him but he was really  brilliant in many many ways and he had  very many good people working for him i  i really view him with ambivalence  ronald reagan  well now there was another friend and  nancy especially i was new before they  were in the white house i think you  never get to be friends with a president  if you don't know them before  because their power is so great and they  see people as  as strangers so um i thought he was  he was a leader he we knew what he stood  for  i think he was very dignified man  i wasn't particularly i didn't agree  with him or all that he did  but nancy was a good friend and still is  bill clinton  i think this well now we're starting on  a second term and  um with both  its promises and its failures  um i'm very hopeful that things are  going to go well and i certainly hope  they do and think that they're many good  signs you say you don't see much of him  you haven't well again he's another  generation  they're very polite and we're not and i  i've seen them a little bit and we have  a mutual friend vernon jordan through  whom i've seen them a little bit  but there's no reason that i should see  them  toughest part of writing your book  it was all  sort of tough because i'm not a  professional writer  and yet i really didn't want to write it  with a writer because i thought that  i would get too jumpy and want to tell  them what to do too much and so i  thought i'd try to do it myself so it  was really hard just to do it where did  you write it  at home on my desk and and  i wrote it by hand  by hand yes  no then what'd you do with it after you  wrote it out i gave it to my researcher  who put it on a computer and put it all  in order and helped me  shape it and helped me make chapters of  it  and helped me get it together very he  was fairly essential and then we sent it  off to the editor who left half of it on  the cutting room floor that's what i  wanted to ask you next it's 625 pages if  they put it all in that you wrote  i think it might have been 1200  is there a second book to this thing no  that's it are you looking forward to  your tour around the country  sort of  would you  what do you think of the talking part of  this  it's hard for me  it's much harder than having written it  why i don't know why but your voice and  your  and talking about it in public  uh i find very hard and i'm not very  good at it either  as you can now see  catherine graham is our guest  and the book is called personal history  thank you very much  and tomorrow an encore q a with  washington post reporter david finkel on  his book thank you for your service
 

